{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3184","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3184","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3184\",\"parent_revision\":\"015a0b9d2094b0f69736512a28e64a7766a0619a\",\"root_revision\":\"6f26f5a0d529c53611669d09f55e455d31ad7c42f758f8de9f775bec93215d80\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P1\\ntype: Bug\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-23\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nThe `test-e2e` job in `.github/workflows/code-quality.yml` is documented as writing to a sandbox. Its own comment block reads:\\n\\n> Creates real GitHub issues in a test sandbox repo and cleans up after.\\n\\nDirectly beneath that comment, the job sets `GITHUB_REPO: Jamie-BitFlight/claude_skills` \u2014 this repository. There is no sandbox repo. The 11 tests in `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_live_validation.py` create real issues in the live tracker, apply status labels to them, rewrite their bodies, sync them, and close them.\\n\\nA comment that contradicts the value on the line below it is worse than no comment. Anyone auditing the workflow reads \\\"sandbox\\\" and stops looking.\\n\\n## Impact\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nTest issues land in the same tracker as real work. They are titled `[MCP-TEST-<UUID>]` and cleaned up two ways \u2014 fixture teardown, then a post-job sweep script \u2014 but both cleanups are best-effort:\\n\\n- The sweep step is `continue-on-error: true`, so a failed cleanup does not fail the job or surface anywhere.\\n- Cleanup matches on title prefix. An issue whose title is not written as expected, or which is created by a code path that does not apply the prefix, is not swept.\\n- Issues are closed, not deleted. Every run leaves closed issues in the repository's history, in search results, and in the numbering sequence.\\n\\nBecause the tests exercise `backlog_add`, `backlog_update`, `backlog_groom`, `backlog_sync` and `backlog_close` against real data, a defect in any of those write paths mutates production issues rather than sandbox ones. This is the same class of problem as #3178, where two scripts mass-overwrite real issue bodies from a stale cache.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Session observation during E2E design review, 2026-08-23\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- **Added**: 2026-08-23\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Observations\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- Workflow job definition: `.github/workflows/code-quality.yml`, the `test-e2e` job \u2014 comment block and `env.GITHUB_REPO` are adjacent and contradictory.\\n- The cleanup script `plugins/development-harness/scripts/close_test_issues.py` scans open issues for the `[MCP-TEST-` prefix.\\n- The emergency cleanup step passes `REPO: ${{ github.repository }}` while the test step passes `GITHUB_REPO: Jamie-BitFlight/claude_skills` \u2014 two different mechanisms naming the target repository, which agree today only because the workflow runs in that repository.\\n- The module docstring in `test_live_validation.py` documents a run command with a path that no longer exists (`.claude/skills/backlog/tests/...`).\\n</div>\\n\\n## Not Established\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z-0</sub>\\n\\nWhether any orphaned `[MCP-TEST-*]` issues currently exist in the repository, and how many runs have left residue. Whether a sandbox repository was ever created and the variable later repointed, or whether the comment was aspirational from the start.\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z-1</sub>\\n\\nWhether any orphaned `[MCP-TEST-*]` issues currently exist in the repository, and how many runs have left residue. Whether a sandbox repository was ever created and the variable later repointed, or whether the comment was aspirational from the start.\\n</div>\\n\\n## User Provided Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z-0</sub>\\n\\nTwo directions are visible but both are design work for grooming, not requirements: point `GITHUB_REPO` at a genuine sandbox repository so the comment becomes true, or keep the production target and make the contradiction explicit. If a sandbox is chosen, the token, permissions and cleanup-script target all have to move with it, and the tests' value as a check against the real backend needs re-examining \u2014 testing against a sandbox is not the same assurance as testing against the repository the tool is actually used on.\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z-1</sub>\\n\\nTwo directions are visible but both are design work for grooming, not requirements: point `GITHUB_REPO` at a genuine sandbox repository so the comment becomes true, or keep the production target and make the contradiction explicit. If a sandbox is chosen, the token, permissions and cleanup-script target all have to move with it, and the tests' value as a check against the real backend needs re-examining \u2014 testing against a sandbox is not the same assurance as testing against the repository the tool is actually used on.\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"c86563b73c47ccfe2991a3c9db319d9b8bd5e084c84c5fe8837a52de01b87ac0\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABQTR-cw\"}"}